# bones event log v1
# fields: timestamp	agent	type	item_id	data
1783539180639200	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExAQ		item.create	bn-3hk	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe umbrella goal for building mess v1: a Rust event-sourcing store with stellar DX backed by a custom crash-proven storage engine. The complete, authoritative design lives in notes/mess-research/12_convergence.md (D1-D11 + A/R/G/F rules), validated and measured by 15 spikes across 4 rounds (docs 13-16, code under spikes/).\n\n# Goals\nShip a v1 that delivers the north-star API — store.command(id, cmd).await? — on the Meridian storage engine, hitting the measured performance envelope in doc 12 (121k+ durable ev/s, ~2M buffered, 95M ev/s sealed replay, 30.8 B/event at rest) with the correctness story (crash, torn-write, handoff) enforced by CI harnesses.\n\n# Reasoning\nEvery design decision here was adversarially reviewed (doc 11), converged (doc 12), spiked, and measured. The phases below follow doc 12's revised roadmap: semantics spec first, DX proven on the boring backend, then the custom log with its crash harness as co-requisite, then index/subscriptions, then verification/compression, then ops.\n\n# How to navigate\nPhase goals are children of this bone; tasks are children of phases. Phase ordering is wired as blocking deps. Every task cites its spec section (D/A/R/G/F numbers) and its spike ancestor (spikes/<name>/ + REPORT.md) — the spikes are working reference implementations, not pseudocode.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All phase goals complete\n- [ ] Envelope regression suite green at doc-12 numbers (with documented tolerance)\n- [ ] Crash/torn-write/handoff harnesses green in CI","kind":"goal","size":"xl","title":"mess v1 — event store per convergence spec"}	blake3:eaMlSHWBnMtbDEXczaRs5dM3MlAI6DT4sgenCjSNY7g
1783539241625179	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExAg		item.create	bn-2um	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe repo predates v1: mess_db/ and mess_ecs/ sit at the root, the sqlx backend is dead,\nand .cargo/config.toml hard-requires lld which isn't installed (every spike carries a\nworkaround). v1 introduces several new crates; the standard crates/ layout keeps them sane.\n\n# Goals\nA clean cargo workspace under crates/ where existing code keeps building and new v1 crates\n(mess-core, mess-log, mess-index, mess-store, mess-derive, mess-testkit) have homes.\n\n# Reasoning\nDo this before Phase 1 so new code lands in its final home; after would mean churn across\nevery open branch. Existing mess_db stays alive through Phase 2-3 (it's the interim backend\nand the RocksDB benchmark baseline), so it moves rather than dies.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All existing crates under crates/, workspace builds, all tests green\n- [ ] New empty v1 crates scaffolded and wired in\n- [ ] Fresh clone builds without manual linker surgery\n","kind":"goal","labels":["infra"],"parent":"bn-3hk","size":"m","title":"Phase R: Workspace restructure to crates/ layout"}	blake3:_t4D-C0cjaTv810Qm_D313gldGRGHB3soWvx38Mn01Q
1783539241726910	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExAw		item.create	bn-22y	{"description":"# Context & Background\nDoc 12 (notes/mess-research/12_convergence.md) is authoritative but is a decision record,\nnot an implementable spec: rules are scattered across amendment blocks, two design items\n(G6, G10) are explicitly unresolved, and byte-exact encodings exist only in spike code.\n\n# Goals\nA docs/spec/ tree that an implementer (or agent) can build Phase 3-5 from without reading\nthe research history: commit semantics + on-disk format, durability/cursors, registry\nbootstrap, fold certificates (with G6/G10 RESOLVED), subscription handoff.\n\n# Reasoning\nThe spikes proved the design but each froze its own dialect of the format. Phase 3 needs\none normative format. This is mostly extraction from doc 12 + spike REPORTs — sized\naccordingly — except the fold-certificate section, which requires real design decisions.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every A1-A12, R1-R4, F-rule, and D-section has a normative home in docs/spec/\n- [ ] G6 and G10 resolved with rationale recorded\n- [ ] Byte-layout tables for BatchHeader/EventSubframe/CommitMarker with CRC coverage defined\n","kind":"goal","labels":["spec"],"parent":"bn-3hk","size":"l","title":"Phase 0: Semantics spec"}	blake3:_8uf_kNxjHiu4crbaOQyJYCmOxnPxRgPDS_IkMAC1Eo
1783539241829588	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExBA		item.create	bn-ix5	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe product surface: Event/Aggregate/Decide traits, EventStore::command() with retry,\nmsgpack-named codec + upcasters, derive macros, Given-When-Then kit. Ground truth exists:\nspikes/dx_api hand-writes exactly what the derives must generate; spikes/codec_bakeoff\nsettled the codec and prototyped the upcaster chain.\n\n# Goals\nThe north-star calling code working against the existing RocksDB actor backend, with the\nboilerplate absorbed by #[derive(Event)] / #[derive(Aggregate)].\n\n# Reasoning\nDoc 12 roadmap: prove DX before custom storage. The API layer is backend-agnostic by\nconstruction (it worked on the interim backend in the spike), so nothing here blocks on\nPhase 3.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] store.command::<Account, _>(id, Withdraw{..}).await? works end-to-end\n- [ ] Derive expansion tested against the dx_api hand-written ground truth\n- [ ] 8-writer concurrency test passes via bounded jittered retry\n","kind":"goal","labels":["dx"],"parent":"bn-3hk","size":"xl","title":"Phase 1: DX first — traits, codec, derives, test kit"}	blake3:H4vhlv2gEWDL2imrt1om9UKjan_aT6eLcH24Bsrm5f0
1783539241932164	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExBQ		item.create	bn-s2a	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe existing mess_db RocksDB actor is the interim backend while the custom engine is built.\nA hardening pass already landed (panic paths, read options, CACHED_GLOBAL fix, growable\nserializer); what remains are the API-shape gaps the dx_api spike catalogued.\n\n# Goals\nThe interim backend is correct enough to run real examples: atomic multi-event append,\nExpectedVersion::Any, paged reads past 10k events, and the legacy decisions (Relaxed/HLC,\nsqlite) closed out.\n\n# Reasoning\nPhase 1's DX layer needs a truthful backend under it for months. Multi-event atomicity is\na correctness gap TODAY (N chained CAS writes = torn appends); it's cheap to fix with one\nWriteBatch. Deliberately NOT ported: batch framing, group commit — that's Phase 3's job.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Multi-event decide() results commit atomically\n- [ ] A 50k-event aggregate loads via paging\n- [ ] StreamPos::Relaxed / HLC / rusqlite decisions recorded and executed\n","kind":"goal","labels":["interim-backend"],"parent":"bn-3hk","size":"m","title":"Phase 2: Boring backend — prove the API on the RocksDB actor"}	blake3:YiwCqdoomidNkEBjTmeqhD_C9B6--SNyC4m8L0PHegM
1783539242039635	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExBg		item.create	bn-2lq	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe Meridian log: batch-framed append-only segments, log-as-sole-commit-authority (D1/D2,\nacceptance rules A1-A12), the final group-commit design (D7), 256 MiB segments. Reference\nimplementations exist and are crash-proven: spikes/crash_log, torn_write, recovery_scale,\nperf_group_commit (seglog.rs + committer), vertical_slice.\n\n# Goals\ncrates/mess-log: production segment writer, committer, and recovery scanner — with the\ncrash harness as a CO-REQUISITE, not a follow-up. The harness ports run in CI from the\nfirst commit of this phase.\n\n# Reasoning\nThe harness-first rule is the project's soul (doc 12 roadmap correction): the spikes'\nrandomized crash loops found A1 and A9 — bugs no unit test would catch. The exit gate is\nquantitative: 10k crash cases green + bench vs the Phase 2 backend.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] 10,000+ randomized crash/recovery cases green in CI (failpoint + sector-reorder + SIGKILL)\n- [ ] Durable append >=100k ev/s and buffered >=1M ev/s on the reference workload\n- [ ] Recovery: fast path <=0.5s; full scan <=0.5 s/GiB cold\n","kind":"goal","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-3hk","size":"xl","title":"Phase 3: Custom log + crash harness"}	blake3:k2hd2blqQsrAhV0hvZAkjmKeoSorXmUyDMZ0Gupq__s
1783539242146972	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExBw		item.create	bn-2jv	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEverything above the log that is a rebuildable cache: the in-memory active index persisted\nat seal (round-4 default, 4.7x append win), the background sealer producing packed pointer\nblocks + BinaryFuse16 filters, fjall metadata tables, the event-sourced $registry (D3),\nsnapshots (snapshot_head O(1)), sealed read paths, and the D11 subscription runtime.\nReferences: spikes/perf_append (sealer/actor), perf_replay (read paths, skip tables),\nseal_pipeline, sub_handoff, ptr_index.\n\n# Goals\nThe full read/write engine composed: EventStore runs on mess-log + mess-index instead of\nthe interim RocksDB backend, including live subscriptions with the proven gapless protocol.\n\n# Reasoning\nIndex is a cache (I5): every structure here must be destroyable and rebuilt from the log —\nthat invariant is what makes the in-memory active index and journal-buffered fjall\ndurability legitimate.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Kill -9 during load: recovery rebuilds active index for every unsealed segment (F6 rule)\n- [ ] Sealed stream replay >=2.5M ev/s; sub_handoff property suite (5k+ scenarios) green\n- [ ] $registry bootstrap: a log restored from bytes alone resolves all names/IDs/dicts\n","kind":"goal","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-3hk","size":"xl","title":"Phase 4: Index/cache engine + subscriptions"}	blake3:szgzXlGk0c8MjLpnLXjUZvGuUqNfQK9mdsiMnJfqEDY
1783539242254574	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExCA		item.create	bn-2fc	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe two seal-time value-adds: fold certificates (D4 — validated at ~165ns/event by\nspikes/fold_cert, pending the G6/G10 spec resolutions from Phase 0) and columnar\ncompression (D6 — the round-4 default tier: columnar/128/zstd-9, 30.8 B/event, byte-exact\nreassembly proven on 1M events by spikes/perf_compress).\n\n# Goals\nload_verified() with honest semantics; sealed segments in the columnar default tier with\nrow fallback; retention rules that never orphan a certificate.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All 13 fold_cert attack scenarios detected in the production impl\n- [ ] Sealed tier <=35 B/event on the reference corpus at >=2.5M ev/s replay\n- [ ] Derive-generated fold-drift golden test fails on a semantic fold change\n","kind":"goal","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-3hk","size":"l","title":"Phase 5: Verification + compression"}	blake3:xSQJdiDTyJMy-KtfZaWz0Pc6u1q8NrMDfwiRqYOzXAY
1783539242363542	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExCQ		item.create	bn-2sk	{"description":"# Context & Background\nOperational excellence is half the product pitch (\"boring operation\"). Doc 09 Phase 10\nlists the CLI; round 4 made the fsync-latency metric MANDATORY (measured 50x fdatasync\ndegradation on a near-full consumer SSD — the store must surface this, not the user).\n\n# Goals\nmess CLI (doctor/inspect/verify/rebuild-index/retention explain), runtime metrics, the\nenvelope regression bench suite as a permanent CI fixture, and benchmark-gated experiments\n(perfect-hash directories, O_DSYNC autodetect) behind evidence.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] mess verify --full detects every corruption class the harnesses can inject\n- [ ] fsync p50/p95/p99 exposed and alarmed\n- [ ] Envelope suite runs in CI with recorded tolerances\n","kind":"goal","labels":["ops"],"parent":"bn-3hk","size":"m","title":"Phase 6: Ops DX + accelerators"}	blake3:p2Y-RyaUD_TEAysGxBwGJBE7Of5U3-avxaMV5ilUH_w
1783539525809255	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExCg		item.create	bn-2gq	{"description":"# Context\nmess_db/ and mess_ecs/ live at repo root; workspace members are an explicit list in the\nroot Cargo.toml. examples/social and xtask stay where they are (or move to a consistent\nspot — implementer's call, document it).\n\n# Approach\ngit mv mess_db -> crates/mess-db, mess_ecs -> crates/mess-ecs; update [workspace] members\nand [workspace.dependencies] path entries; rg for any hardcoded relative paths (spikes\nreference ../../mess_db — spikes are frozen artifacts, do NOT chase them; note breakage in\nspikes/README if needed). Decide dash-vs-underscore package naming and stay consistent.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] cargo build && cargo test -p mess_db green from a clean checkout\n- [ ] git history preserved through the move (git log --follow works)\n- [ ] Root crate `mess` still re-exports and builds\n","kind":"task","labels":["infra"],"parent":"bn-2um","size":"m","title":"Move existing crates into crates/ and fix workspace"}	blake3:ANqsz-_D356OG-SgMDvao4OhJcJiZdTPHHLx9rPHI_w
1783539525915949	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExCw		item.create	bn-1i1	{"description":"# Context\nv1 code needs final homes before Phase 1 starts. Crate boundaries follow the design's\nreplaceable seams (doc 12 / doc 01 traits): core types+traits / segment log / index+seal /\nengine composition / proc macros / test kit.\n\n# Approach\nEmpty lib crates under crates/ with one-line doc headers stating each crate's charter and\nwhich spec sections it implements. mess-derive is proc-macro=true. Wire into workspace,\nCI, and justfile targets.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All six crates build and are workspace members\n- [ ] Each crate's lib.rs doc header names its spec sections (D-numbers)\n","kind":"task","labels":["infra"],"parent":"bn-2um","size":"s","title":"Scaffold v1 crates: mess-core, mess-log, mess-index, mess-store, mess-derive, mess-testkit"}	blake3:0g6u1DmfKM2VMjx3O7Xtlg6WbrqH1nNvK36OJ9FuCT0
1783539526019005	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExDA		item.create	bn-igp	{"description":"# Context\n.cargo/config.toml hard-requires -fuse-ld=lld; lld is not installed on this machine.\nEvery one of the 15 spikes carries a local workaround — that's the smell.\n\n# Approach\nEither install lld and document the dependency, or gate the fast-linker flags behind an\noptional config (e.g. keep them in a committed config.toml.example / use rustflags via\njustfile). A fresh clone must build with stock toolchain.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] cargo build succeeds on a machine without lld\n- [ ] Spike-local .cargo workarounds no longer necessary (leave the frozen spikes as-is)\n","kind":"task","labels":["infra"],"parent":"bn-2um","size":"xs","title":"Make the workspace build without lld installed"}	blake3:8j0PQu0ZFD1dMuTWsZ9RZls13-xvweJjfeFyjYerwxE
1783539526118576	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExDQ		item.create	bn-21c	{"description":"# Context\nThe normative core: log-with-commit-markers as sole authority, batch framing, all twelve\nacceptance rules, recovery rules, 256 MiB segments. Everything exists in doc 12 amendment\nblocks + spike code (crash_log format v1, vertical_slice format v2, torn_write) but there\nare now TWO dialects of the format in spike code — the spec must pick one normative layout.\n\n# Approach\ndocs/spec/01-log-format.md + 02-recovery.md. Byte-layout tables for BatchHeader /\nEventSubframe (incl. schema_version u16, dict_id u16, codec_id) / CommitMarker; CRC split\ncoverage (R4 — hash AROUND checksum fields, never copy-and-zero); segment header/footer\nincl. A9 epoch (R3); recovery algorithm as pseudocode with the A-rules inline where they\nbite; parallel-scan allowance (R1) and manifest footer cache (R2).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every A1-A12 and R1-R4 rule appears exactly once, normatively\n- [ ] A clean-room implementer could produce byte-identical output from the tables alone\n- [ ] Torn-write fault model documented as the conformance bar (spikes/torn_write = 24k cases)\n","kind":"task","labels":["spec"],"parent":"bn-22y","size":"m","title":"Spec: commit semantics and on-disk format (D1/D2, A1-A12, R1-R4)"}	blake3:vtBrD11hn-qJ5CAIyQxRSuU6DLIJN3a_4TllLUgQlwE
1783539526221044	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExDg		item.create	bn-30c	{"description":"# Context\nRound 4 finalized the committer design: single file, single committer thread, early-close\nwindow (max_delay cap + max_bytes close), ONE coalesced write per group, position-ordered\ndurable watermark acks, O_DSYNC as device-verified option, striping rejected (documented).\nCursorRegressed and the A6 recovered-but-unacked mirror are the API-visible consequences.\n\n# Approach\ndocs/spec/03-durability.md from doc 12 D7 + spikes/perf_group_commit/REPORT.md. Include\nthe measured knee (~1.5 MB/group) as design rationale, the convoy-split race the central\ncommitter fixes, and the never-hold-append-across-barrier rule.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each Durability mode's loss window and ack meaning stated precisely\n- [ ] CursorRegressed and A6 defined as typed, documented outcomes\n- [ ] Striping rejection + revisit conditions recorded\n","kind":"task","labels":["spec"],"parent":"bn-22y","size":"s","title":"Spec: durability modes, group commit, cursor semantics (D7)"}	blake3:JJBhzsLbSsIa4dcvWwWyZ6fgRRi4MCAeSDO-q3cGZYs
1783539526327291	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExDw		item.create	bn-1rq	{"description":"# Context\nInterned IDs break rebuildability (I5) unless the registry itself is event-sourced.\nDoc 12 D3 has the rules: reserved IDs (stream 0 = $registry, event_type 0, category 0,\ncodec 0 FROZEN FOREVER), alias-not-rename, no compaction in v1, DictRegistered entries.\n\n# Approach\ndocs/spec/04-registry.md. Define the registry event types' frozen codec-0 encodings\nbyte-exactly (they can never change — evolution is new event types only). Specify writer-\nassigned ID allocation and the recovery order (registry replays before anything needs it).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Bootstrap decode path has no circular dependency (provable from the doc)\n- [ ] Dict lifecycle: registered before referenced; never deleted while referenced\n","kind":"task","labels":["spec"],"parent":"bn-22y","size":"s","title":"Spec: registry bootstrap and dictionaries (D3)"}	blake3:jR3PUm2DusFbFRckE2xzWERz5wal-D3z87YSCSbblcU
1783539526434850	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExEA		item.create	bn-oc3	{"description":"# Context\nThe one Phase 0 item that is real design work, not extraction. spikes/fold_cert validated\nthe mechanism (~165ns/event, 13/13 attacks detected) but found the spec unimplementable:\nG6 — the trusted head anchor must live OUTSIDE the rebuildable index (candidates: segment\nfooter, manifest) or truncation detection anchors to nothing; G10 — where h[i-1] lives in\nthe BATCH format (per-frame = ~25% overhead on small events; per-batch + intra-batch\nrecompute is the likely answer). Plus: unify the genesis formula (docs 08/12 disagree),\nstream_id in genesis is MANDATORY (cross-stream attack demonstrated), byte-exact encodings,\nversion-0/empty-prefix representation, retention must preserve certification frames.\n\n# Approach\ndocs/spec/05-fold-certificates.md. Decide G6 and G10 with rationale (fold_cert REPORT\ngaps 1-10 is the checklist). Both verification paths (A: recompute from frame v; B: read\nframe v+1's chain value) specified with when-each-applies.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] G6 decided: anchor location + its durability/rebuild story\n- [ ] G10 decided: chain storage layout with measured overhead bound\n- [ ] All 10 fold_cert REPORT gaps dispositioned\n- [ ] BLOCKS Phase 5 fold implementation — wired as an explicit dep\n","kind":"task","labels":["spec","design-decision"],"parent":"bn-22y","size":"m","title":"Spec: fold certificates — RESOLVE G6 and G10 (D4)"}	blake3:RhmMgfUEKAAH9tk2WWVh67UBk8ORqWjcEh9XNKErxBo
1783539526543633	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExEQ		item.create	bn-3hp	{"description":"# Context\nThe gapless catch-up->live protocol, proven by spikes/sub_handoff (5,600 scenarios,\n10,617 sequences, 0 anomalies; naive alternative loses events in 291/300 races).\n\n# Approach\ndocs/spec/06-subscriptions.md — near-verbatim from doc 12 D11 + the spike REPORT's spec\nsection. Keep the writer obligation (watermark-before-publish, publish order = position\norder) and the <=-not-== dedupe rule prominent; both are the load-bearing details.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] State machine + invariant stated; overflow = lossy-but-loud rule included\n- [ ] The spike's property suite named as the conformance bar\n","kind":"task","labels":["spec"],"parent":"bn-22y","size":"xs","title":"Spec: subscription handoff (D11)"}	blake3:qZUJtkPYNL4boR_IlLI__USb9GNzZ8UzTqxxLsE9Ctc
1783539526652692	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExEg		item.create	bn-33v	{"description":"# Context\nThe product's core vocabulary. Ground truth: spikes/dx_api/src/lib.rs — plain-trait\nversions proven end-to-end against the real actor backend.\n\n# Approach\nPort the spike traits into crates/mess-core with production error types: DomainError\nbecomes an associated type (type Rejection: Error) per the spike's own note; CodecError,\nCommandError::{Domain, Conflict, Store} keep their spike shapes. No proc macros here —\nderives come later and must expand to impls of THESE traits.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] dx_api's bank_account test compiles against mess-core with hand-written impls\n- [ ] Aggregate::apply is infallible; Decide::decide returns typed rejections\n- [ ] No dependency on any backend crate\n","kind":"task","labels":["dx"],"parent":"bn-ix5","size":"m","title":"mess-core: Event / Aggregate / Decide traits + error taxonomy"}	blake3:BhVThhH_vws62knNqCYoggPqPGuY3hRdqYOZnRfj02Q
1783539526764872	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExEw		item.create	bn-31s	{"description":"# Context\nRound-3 decision (spikes/codec_bakeoff): codec_id 1 = rmp_serde::to_vec_named — the only\nfast evolution-safe codec (zero silent-wrong cells; postcard/bincode/compact-msgpack\ndisqualified by demonstrated silent corruption on same-typed field reorder and enum\nvariant reorder). Upcaster prototype in the spike works with committed byte fixtures.\n\n# Approach\nStoredEvent { event_name, schema_version: u16, codec_id, payload } envelope; Codec trait\nwith codec_id dispatch; Upcast<V_prev> one-hop trait + transitive dispatch (the spike's\nevent_versions! macro shape — hand-rolled here, derive-generated later). Port the\ncodec_bakeoff fixture tests: V1/V2 committed bytes must decode to exact V3 values forever.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Evolution matrix tests ported: the 9 scenarios classify identically to the spike\n- [ ] Unknown version/codec fail loudly with actionable errors\n- [ ] Encoder determinism guard test (same event -> same bytes)\n","kind":"task","labels":["dx"],"parent":"bn-ix5","size":"m","title":"mess-core: codec layer — msgpack-named, StoredEvent envelope, upcaster chain"}	blake3:w6eBWWT1-bC7q23segh0q3ponWcYAVfzUgC-vq7tCQ0
1783539526871272	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExFA		item.create	bn-hy7	{"description":"# Context\nThe north-star API. spikes/dx_api proved it on the real actor: 8 tasks x 25 commands on\none stream -> exact balance via retry (~1,000 attempts, max 24 for one command). The spike\ndefault budget of 16 was measured too small.\n\n# Approach\nBackend trait (append_batch/read_stream/read_global/head) so the facade is engine-agnostic;\nfirst impl wraps the mess-db actor. command() = load -> decide -> append(expected) with\nretry policy { attempts: default > 24, jittered backoff }; typed exhaustion error. Paged\nload loop (removes the 10k ceiling at the API level, backend paging lands in Phase 2).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] dx_api's 8-writer concurrency test ported and green\n- [ ] Retry policy configurable; conflict-exhaustion is a distinct error variant\n- [ ] Facade compiles with a mock backend (no rocksdb) for unit tests\n","kind":"task","labels":["dx"],"parent":"bn-ix5","size":"m","title":"mess-store: EventStore facade — load / append / command with bounded jittered retry"}	blake3:7F0W6HzUBHAPjy6eBY81FOGQ_u2nRvUNcKIxA0hTwoM
1783539526978683	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExFQ		item.create	bn-2cn	{"description":"# Context\nThe most-loved DX feature of mature ES frameworks. Store-free ground truth in\nspikes/dx_api/src/testkit.rs with positional-diff panics.\n\n# Approach\nPort to crates/mess-testkit; add .then_error(matcher) ergonomics and readable diffs\n(pretty_assertions or equivalent). This crate later hosts the derive-generated fixture\nand fold-drift tests, so keep its API macro-friendly.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] AggregateTest::given([..]).when(cmd).then_events([..]) / .then_error(..) work store-free\n- [ ] Failure output shows a positional event diff a human can read\n","kind":"task","labels":["dx"],"parent":"bn-ix5","size":"s","title":"mess-testkit: Given-When-Then aggregate test kit"}	blake3:rivNS40D5hnBg1TlTF-zTogx9TqvAQsxcFoLnMuteG0
1783539527086093	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExFg		item.create	bn-1an	{"description":"# Context\nThe derives absorb exactly the boilerplate spikes/dx_api hand-writes (~30 lines/enum:\nname() match, encode/decode plumbing, per-variant name dispatch — the manual impl fudges\nvariant dispatch via serde enum tagging; the derive must dispatch on the stored name).\n\n# Approach\n#[derive(Event)] with #[event(name = \"account\", version = N)]: variant -> \"account.opened\"\nstyle names, codec impls via mess-core codec layer, schema fingerprint per variant.\n#[derive(Aggregate)] is thin (apply stays hand-written — it IS the domain logic).\nExpansion tests: trybuild + a test asserting the derive-generated impl behaves identically\nto dx_api's hand-written ground truth on the same fixtures.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Bank-account example rewritten with derives only, all dx_api tests still green\n- [ ] Wire names stable under variant reorder (name-keyed, not index-keyed)\n- [ ] Helpful compile errors (trybuild UI tests) for missing attrs\n","kind":"task","labels":["dx"],"parent":"bn-ix5","size":"m","title":"mess-derive: #[derive(Event)] and #[derive(Aggregate)]"}	blake3:VJktrkUIbo3z5EogloxACzPwo_oSi_DTsPiqs7Rl_GU
1783539527190484	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExFw		item.create	bn-3ui	{"description":"# Context\nTwo generated test families make evolution and fold_version failures loud at CI time\ninstead of silent at runtime: (1) fixture-compat — committed old-version bytes must decode\nforever (codec_bakeoff pattern); (2) fold-drift — fixture events + expected folded state;\nchanging apply() semantics fails with \"bump fold_version or fix your fold\" (D4 guard,\nprototyped in spikes/fold_cert with the cfg-flagged drift demo).\n\n# Approach\nA #[derive]-adjacent macro or testkit helper that scaffolds fixture files on first run and\nasserts against them thereafter (snapshot-test ergonomics, but committed to the repo).\nfold_version lives in #[aggregate(fold_version = N)].\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Deliberately changing a fold behind a feature flag fails the generated test with the\n      bump-or-fix message (port the fold_cert demo)\n- [ ] Renaming a field without an upcaster fails fixture-compat loudly\n","kind":"task","labels":["dx","verification"],"parent":"bn-ix5","size":"m","title":"mess-derive: generated fixture-compat and fold-drift golden tests"}	blake3:fbkZ9oIJ4fco-SIyem7UPjRVsMKIrUUTWW4M9iw_ALM
1783539527292400	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExGA		item.create	bn-1pc	{"description":"# Context\nDoc 12 roadmap puts snapshots in the Phase 1 API even though the real snapshot store is\nPhase 4: the API shape (load uses snapshot+tail transparently) must exist early so user\ncode never changes when the engine does.\n\n# Approach\nSnapshotRef per doc 12 D4 (incl. fold_version field, event_prefix_hash reserved/optional\nuntil Phase 5); store.save_snapshot::<A>(id) + transparent use in load(). Interim storage:\na snapshots CF or keyspace on the existing backend — explicitly throwaway, correctness via\nthe snapshot law test (snapshot+tail == full replay, property-tested).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Property test: fold(s0, all) == fold(snapshot.state, tail) across random prefixes\n- [ ] fold_version mismatch invalidates and falls back to full replay\n","kind":"task","labels":["dx"],"parent":"bn-ix5","size":"s","title":"Snapshot API surface: SnapshotRef, save/load on the interim backend"}	blake3:wAgJ6F26jjeCHLMlMe7VerN7mKNh-Et0jscE_Xnd-4M
1783539527398836	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExGQ		item.create	bn-b2r	{"description":"# Context\ndx_api friction items #2 and #3: a multi-event decide() result is currently N chained CAS\nwrites (torn appends possible TODAY), and None means only \"stream must be empty,\" forcing\nread-before-write on every append.\n\n# Approach\nExtend the actor write request to Vec<events>: one version check, N records, one\nrocksdb::WriteBatch (atomic). ExpectedVersion enum { NoStream, Exact(v), Any } threaded\nthrough WriteMessage; Any skips the head read. Keep wire compat with existing callers or\nmigrate them — small crate, just do it.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Concurrency test: racing multi-event commands never interleave within a batch\n- [ ] Any-mode append does zero reads before write (assert via metrics/instrumentation)\n","kind":"task","labels":["interim-backend"],"parent":"bn-s2a","size":"m","title":"Atomic multi-event append + ExpectedVersion::Any in mess-db"}	blake3:rxcP54_xHcgYHTXNQnOLNMwGUYEbvE1xrS-My6doHNg
1783539527506993	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExGg		item.create	bn-3ps	{"description":"# Context\nThe hardening pass wired from_stream_position seeks; EventStore::load (Phase 1) loops\npages. This task closes the loop on the backend: verify + test the full paged path and\nkill any remaining single-page assumptions in svc handlers.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A 50,000-event stream loads correctly through EventStore::load\n- [ ] Paging test covers the exact LIMIT_MAX boundary (off-by-one bait)\n","kind":"task","labels":["interim-backend"],"parent":"bn-s2a","size":"s","title":"Backend paging: stream reads beyond LIMIT_MAX"}	blake3:XJhG9oFpT87DCGCLpl5hOxzwjkPYfnbOrj7kO2JInVU
1783539527613174	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExGw		item.create	bn-17h	{"description":"# Context\nThree pieces of pre-research code need explicit disposition (doc 12 Phase 2 mandate: no\nsilent carry). StreamPos's 1-bit Sequential/Relaxed encoding conflicts with the new plain\nu64 stream_version; the HLC clock (rocks/clock.rs) is built+tested but unwired; rusqlite\nis the more-complete backend but the plan drops it.\n\n# Recommendation (decide + record in an ADR, then execute)\nRelaxed: retire the bit-flag encoding; relaxed/causal streams return post-v1 via doc 06's\nalgebraic modes, not this encoding. HLC: delete (git preserves it); revisit with relaxed\nstreams. rusqlite: delete the backend, keep the migration-trigger ideas noted in the ADR.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] ADR committed with the three decisions + rationale\n- [ ] Code matches the ADR; workspace builds; no todo!() paths remain in mess-db\n","kind":"task","labels":["interim-backend","design-decision"],"parent":"bn-s2a","size":"s","title":"Decide and execute: StreamPos::Relaxed, HLC clock, rusqlite backend"}	blake3:WUYeJp-Eq3ylUYkVRmHIzqL7buHzo6DeTWydhavhdpk
1783539527719114	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExHA		item.create	bn-3p5	{"description":"# Context\nexamples/social is the only end-to-end exercise of the old mess_ecs path; the bank account\nlives only in spike tests. Examples are the DX acid test and the docs' backbone.\n\n# Approach\nexamples/bank (from dx_api, with derives) and examples/social rewritten on EventStore.\nRetire mess-ecs's Component/Entity vocabulary in the process (doc 09 Phase 0 renames) —\nmark the crate deprecated or delete per what the social port still needs.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Both examples run with `cargo run --example` against a temp store\n- [ ] Zero references to Entity/Component/ECS vocabulary remain in example code\n","kind":"task","labels":["dx"],"parent":"bn-s2a","size":"s","title":"Port examples to the v1 API: bank account + social"}	blake3:q6MVgvjsogfjco0tu6JUUWcbdr1rQxp91ByqCXYj21Q
1783539527873313	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExHQ		item.create	bn-ise	{"description":"# Context\nThe canonical write path per docs/spec/01: BatchHeader + EventSubframes + CommitMarker,\n256 MiB segments, A5/A7/A8 (no empty batches, batch==segment alignment, no spanning).\nReference impls: spikes/perf_group_commit/src/seglog.rs (format v2, most current) and\nspikes/perf_append (zero-alloc encode path: contiguous batch buffer, one vectored write,\n0.045 syscalls/event).\n\n# Approach\nPort seglog v2 to crates/mess-log AGAINST THE SPEC, not against the spike (the spec is\nnormative where dialects differ). Keep the perf_append encode discipline from day one —\nit was measured, not speculative. Segment roll allocates the next file; sealing is\nPhase 4's job (writer just closes and hands off).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Byte output matches the spec tables (golden-file test)\n- [ ] Encode path: zero allocations per event on the hot path (alloc-counter test)\n- [ ] R4: checksums computed around the checksum fields, no copy-and-zero anywhere\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"m","title":"mess-log: batch encoder + segment writer"}	blake3:rsLWzZ08qYE3M_nhQLnGM9dsBgBclLtEL9oEgzBr8I8
1783539527978738	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExHg		item.create	bn-39n	{"description":"# Context\nRecovery IS the commit-authority definition (D1): accept only marker-terminated,\nCRC-valid, contiguous (A1), current-epoch (A9) batches; stop at the first hole, never\nresync (A10); no CRC-off path may exist (A12). References: spikes/crash_log scanner +\nspikes/torn_write hardened variant + spikes/recovery_scale (mmap scan at device speed,\nR4 split coverage already implemented there).\n\n# Approach\nPort the torn_write-hardened scanner; scan-from-mmap per recovery_scale's finding (the\nfs::read copy was the warm ceiling). Single-threaded first — R1 parallelism is a Phase 4\noptimization once footers exist. Expose the safe-truncation offset and recovered heads.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] crash_log's 19 deterministic edge cases ported and green\n- [ ] Stale-generation (A9) and resync-bait (A10) adversarial cases ported and green\n- [ ] Re-recovery idempotence test\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"m","title":"mess-log: recovery scanner with full acceptance rules"}	blake3:Hfc0DDwoVwtsu1JKsDAhoZbB5IyvEXMSm4Mhm2ZnNVM
1783539528088098	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExHw		item.create	bn-11m	{"description":"# Context\nRound-4 final design (docs/spec/03): single committer thread gathers the group, assigns\npositions centrally, ONE coalesced write, fdatasync, advance position-ordered durable\nwatermark, ack. Early-close + max_bytes + max_delay-as-cap. O_DSYNC as verified option.\nReference: spikes/perf_group_commit/src/engines.rs (the `committer` variants — measured\n121k-363k durable ev/s).\n\n# Approach\nPort the committer engine; Durability::{Buffered, Os, Group{..}} from the spec; the\nwatermark here is the same object D11 subscriptions consume later — design its type for\nthat (watch channel or equivalent). Never hold the append path across the barrier.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Reproduce >=100k durable ev/s at 4 writers x 100-event batches on the reference workload\n- [ ] Early-close beats sync-per-batch at 4 writers (regression-tested ratio)\n- [ ] Acks strictly after covering barrier — verified by the crash harness\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"m","title":"mess-log: committer — group commit, durability modes, watermark acks"}	blake3:PkKmqYeLSco5AR2Gw030x43DWUA4vu24wbPV63PNaBc
1783539528195820	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExIA		item.create	bn-3jg	{"description":"# Context\nCO-REQUISITE of this phase, not a follow-up — the spikes' randomized loops found A1 and\nA9; unit tests never would have. Reference: spikes/crash_log (12k-iteration loop,\nFaultWriter, in-memory reference model).\n\n# Approach\nPort FaultWriter + the randomized loop into mess-log's test suite against the REAL writer/\nscanner/committer. Fast profile (~1-2k cases) on every CI run; full 10k+ profile nightly\nor behind a feature flag. Assertions: acked-implies-recovered, no partial batch visible,\ncontiguous positions after resume, idempotent re-recovery.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Harness runs against production mess-log types (no spike copies of the format)\n- [ ] 10,000+ cases green; seed-reproducible failures print the seed\n- [ ] Wired into CI from this phase's first merge\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","harness"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"m","title":"Crash harness port: failpoints + randomized kill loop + reference model (CI)"}	blake3:DgrQ74gKm22U1FbydZd4QGOvGlzM8miAlaY667pPw9Q
1783539528300913	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExIQ		item.create	bn-py3	{"description":"# Context\nThe A4 conformance bar: 24,000 sector-reorder cases, differential proof that the batch\nCRC is load-bearing (348 wrongly-accepted batches with it disabled). Reference:\nspikes/torn_write (SectorDisk fault model + matrix).\n\n# Approach\nPort SectorDisk as a mess-log test utility; run the matrix (512B/4KiB sectors x zeros/\ngarbage/stale backgrounds) against the production scanner. Keep the differential mode as\na compile-time-gated test proving A12 stays load-bearing (it must FAIL when CRC checks\nare stubbed out — a test of the test).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Full-validation matrix green at >=20k cases (nightly profile)\n- [ ] Differential mode demonstrates wrongly-accepted batches when CRC disabled\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","harness"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"m","title":"Torn-write / sector-reorder harness port (CI)"}	blake3:sNxWLxOAS3TMH_fpf4q6ldCXt1MR6gTC39_aH23DdLg
1783539528402618	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExIg		item.create	bn-2iq	{"description":"# Context\nReal-process kills catch what simulated fault layers can't (buffering surprises between\nthe process and the kernel). Reference: spikes/recovery_scale (5-kill harness) and\nperf_group_commit (10 rounds at aggressive configs, 34,187 batches verified).\n\n# Approach\nTest binary: spawn a child writing under a chosen Durability mode, SIGKILL at random\noffsets, recover, assert acked-durable-survives per the mode's contract (the child\nreports acks over a pipe before dying).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] 10+ kill rounds per durability mode in the nightly profile\n- [ ] Ack ledger vs recovered set reconciles exactly per mode contract\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","harness"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"s","title":"SIGKILL child-writer harness"}	blake3:oki-LvuouIKUtLksHXOb15xJd0A0Dr-D_opsRdR4cE4
1783539528508240	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExIw		item.create	bn-12d	{"description":"# Context\nReaders must never scan past the committed watermark into in-flight bytes (D7); this\nwatermark is also D11's subscription source and A6's boundary. The committer task\nproduces it; this task makes it consumable: shared handle, ordering guarantees\n(watermark-before-publish is the D11 writer obligation).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Reader at the watermark sees exactly the committed prefix during active writes\n      (concurrent property test)\n- [ ] Watermark handle exposes await-past(position) for subscriptions\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"s","title":"Committed watermark + active-segment reader coordination"}	blake3:EMAb4411Eeygqstj3oZf-RDxDAWFo_3IReep3GRWzkA
1783539528614555	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExJA		item.create	bn-gke	{"description":"# Context\nD9: one writer process per store dir, OS lock, no cleverness (RocksDB's rule).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Second open of the same dir fails with a clear typed error\n- [ ] Lock released on clean close AND stale-lock recovery after SIGKILL documented/tested\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"xs","title":"Store lock file: single-writer-process enforcement"}	blake3:ULEEEnRkwRPEKf6nXEWRYOQyi47_PE_DV767mnh14ug
1783539528720335	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExJQ		item.create	bn-25e	{"description":"# Context\nD8 / fsyncgate: on fsync EIO the page cache state is unknowable — poison writes, serve\nreads only in an explicit Degraded state, require restart+recovery. Never retry-and-continue.\n\n# Approach\nPoison flag on the committer; all write paths check it; reads return values tagged/\nqueryable as degraded. Test via a failpoint that injects EIO from the fault layer.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Injected EIO: subsequent appends fail fast with StorePoisoned; reads still work\n      but store reports Degraded\n- [ ] Restart runs recovery and clears the state\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"s","title":"fsync-EIO poisoning + degraded reads"}	blake3:F_AcEddWagmiMSPja2RDVmmHKcfsGpFj3LuetkAb7WE
1783539528825966	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExJg		item.create	bn-sbt	{"description":"# Context\nA9 made the segment epoch mandatory BEFORE any recycling; R3 puts the epoch in the footer;\nA7/A8 pin batch/segment alignment. Sealed footers are written by Phase 4's sealer — this\ntask defines the footer type + the epoch/manifest plumbing and the (v1: delete-only,\nnever-recycle) policy.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Epoch monotonic per segment slot; scanner rejects stale-epoch batches (A9 test)\n- [ ] v1 policy recorded: segments are deleted, never recycled in place\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"s","title":"Segment lifecycle: roll, epoch stamping, footer, recycling rules"}	blake3:s7ktfhdhu9aUfXrLp4hQe0gr493SaiFxt-NggMkLCm8
1783539528932833	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExJw		item.create	bn-2m1	{"description":"# Context\nThe quantitative gate from doc 12's roadmap. The soul-of-the-project milestone\n(doc 09): correctness proven by volume, performance proven against the Phase 2 backend.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All three harnesses green at full profiles in one CI run\n- [ ] Bench: durable >=100k ev/s (4x100), buffered >=1M ev/s vs interim backend numbers\n      recorded side-by-side\n- [ ] Results appended to the envelope ledger (seed of the Phase 6 regression suite)\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","gate"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"s","title":"Phase 3 exit gate: 10k crash cases + bench vs interim backend"}	blake3:DlH7Dpft1d0oPwIsCD7bGdK20QrCsXMZ_C2GiXbPAUE
1783539529037211	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExKA		item.create	bn-25d	{"description":"# Context\nRound-4 default (4.7x composed append win): the active index lives in memory and persists\nonly at seal. Consequence (F6 rule): recovery rebuilds the active index for EVERY segment\nlacking a sealed footer — a rolled-but-unsealed segment is normal under async sealing.\nReference: spikes/perf_append (the actor-pipe engine that hit 1.71M ev/s).\n\n# Approach\nPer-stream Vec<EventPtr> map + stream heads; populated by the committer pipeline;\nrecovery path replays unsealed segments through the same insertion code (one code path,\nnot two). Memory bound: one segment's worth of pointers (~1M events ≈ tens of MB) per\nunsealed segment.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Kill -9 with 2 unsealed segments: both rebuilt, heads exact (harness case)\n- [ ] Composed buffered append >=1M ev/s at 4 writers with index maintenance on\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"m","title":"mess-index: in-memory active index + unsealed-segment rebuild on recovery"}	blake3:4hAspcaMmReeKDWNzjwJKg6HELXwfWc3OsfuCtsq1lU
1783539529145675	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExKQ		item.create	bn-20e	{"description":"# Context\nThe seal pass turns write-optimized into read-optimized off the hot path (D5). References:\nspikes/seal_pipeline (consolidation, variable-length varint-delta blocks), perf_replay\n(intra-block skip table: point-read p99 108us -> 0.79us for +0.16 B/event), perf_append\n(sealer thread removes the 1.4s inline roll stall — invisible below full segment scale,\nso TEST AT FULL SCALE).\n\n# Approach\nDedicated sealer thread consuming rolled segments: consolidate active-index entries ->\npacked blocks with skip tables -> footer (epoch, summaries, offsets). Compression enters\nin Phase 5 — structure the sealer as staged passes so the columnar stage slots in.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Seal of a full 256 MiB segment completes off the append path (no append-latency\n      spike during roll — measured)\n- [ ] Sealed stream replay >= pre-seal pointer-chase on the same data\n- [ ] Point-read p99 through skip tables <2us cached\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"m","title":"mess-index: background sealer — packed pointer blocks, skip tables, footer"}	blake3:y9C9KUj_xSwlKmbZPW8iXwzyGKkcD0L1DkUD_QEzn7Q
1783539529252485	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExKg		item.create	bn-1ku	{"description":"# Context\nThe exact-KV tables (doc 04): stream_head, snapshot_head, projection checkpoints, recent\ndedupe. Backend decided by spike ptr_index: fjall at journal-buffered durability —\nlegitimate because all of it is rebuildable from the log (I5).\n\n# Approach\nOne fjall keyspace per table; writes batched per commit group (F1 lesson — never per\nevent). Rebuild-from-log path for each table (this is I5 paying rent — test it by\ndeleting the fjall dir and recovering). Dedupe window semantics per spec (extent +\nA6 retry interaction).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] rm -rf the metadata dir -> full rebuild from log, byte-equal table contents\n- [ ] Dedupe: duplicate message_id within window returns original position; A6 case tested\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"m","title":"mess-index: metadata tables on fjall — heads, checkpoints, dedupe window"}	blake3:rhZp2cx3uE_5XY9Fo3eIEuN7CVpakYI1mTWwEEGyvTc
1783539529365259	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExKw		item.create	bn-2py	{"description":"# Context\nD3 / docs/spec/04: interned IDs with the registry event-sourced into the log itself.\nReserved IDs (stream 0, event_type 0, category 0, codec 0 frozen), alias-not-rename,\nwriter-assigned allocation, DictRegistered for Phase 5 dictionaries.\n\n# Approach\nRegistry events use frozen codec 0 (postcard is FINE here — frozen internal structs,\nnever evolved; evolution = new event types). In-memory maps hydrated by replaying\n$registry first during open; first-use of a new stream/type/category appends the\nregistration in the same commit group as the first event.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Restore-from-bytes-alone test: open a copied log dir, all names resolve (I5 proof)\n- [ ] Codec-0 encodings golden-file tested (they can never change)\n- [ ] Rename produces an alias event; old name still resolves\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"m","title":"$registry stream: bootstrap, name/ID resolution, dict registration"}	blake3:Benf5WqWNSjjvwSlPK5DYBBbU6OA39I9NIhd5_-xc7Q
1783539529476486	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExLA		item.create	bn-1i7	{"description":"# Context\nRound-3 measured pick: BinaryFuse16 (0.002% FPR, 3.61 B/key, ~26 KiB/segment, ~4ns query,\nzero false negatives) for stream/category membership per sealed segment. xorf crate.\nCorrectness rule: filters only ever skip work — no: definitely absent; maybe: check exact.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Filter says no -> segment skipped in stream replay planning; measured FPR in test\n      matches the ~0.002% band\n- [ ] Filter corruption/absence degrades to exact-index fallback (never wrong answers)\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"s","title":"Seal-time membership filters: BinaryFuse16"}	blake3:XUvG3z4liQlRFozxNdVFf1Bg5nzeaVuznoAGWYFJFnU
1783539529581527	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExLQ		item.create	bn-14l	{"description":"# Context\nThe proven gapless protocol (docs/spec/06): subscribe-first, page history to empty, drain\nlive with <=-dedupe, lossy-but-loud overflow -> regress to catch-up. Reference:\nspikes/sub_handoff (protocol + 5,600-scenario property suite + the naive counterexample).\n\n# Approach\nImplement over the real committed watermark (Phase 3 handle) and real paged reads;\nbroadcast buffer sized by commit-batch size (spike finding). Port the ENTIRE property\nsuite against the real engine — it's the conformance bar. Surface CursorRegressed to\nconsumers (never silently absorbed).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] 5,000+ randomized scenarios green against the real engine\n- [ ] Overflow storm test: one Lagged -> stable catch-up, no flapping, no gaps\n- [ ] Subscription lag metric (watermark - cursor) exposed\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","dx"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"m","title":"Subscription runtime per D11 + ported property suite"}	blake3:-V1fVu6X7ov3-imXSwGxUeJUa82r-H-T8bFbvN2zb1w
1783539529687925	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExLg		item.create	bn-fdv	{"description":"# Context\nThe load path promise: snapshot_head lookup -> blob read -> tail replay (doc 05).\nReplaces the Phase 1 interim snapshot storage; the API surface must not change (that was\nthe point of doing the API first).\n\n# Approach\nSnapshot blobs in their own append-only blob file(s) with CRC; snapshot_head +\nsnapshot_by_version in the fjall metadata tables; monotonic head CAS; corruption falls\nback to previous snapshot then full replay (never poison the head permanently). Policy:\nevery-N-events manual/opt-in for v1 (adaptive policy is post-v1).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Aggregate load = head lookup + blob + tail (no scan) — instrumented test\n- [ ] Corrupt-latest-snapshot test: falls back and self-heals per doc 05 rules\n- [ ] Phase 1 property test (snapshot law) still green unchanged\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","dx"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"m","title":"Snapshot store: snapshot_head O(1) + EventStore integration"}	blake3:UjT2zzmUoiSGiUja3rX-XrhuCJaNUWqcVixBgtvO178
1783539529794744	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExLw		item.create	bn-1hx	{"description":"# Context\nThe measured read designs from spikes/perf_replay: parallel block decompress (95M ev/s\nglobal), segment-order coalesced preads + parallel decompress for streams (24.6M ev/s),\ndecompressed-block LRU (48% hit on replay; useless for random point reads — rely on skip\ntables there). Until Phase 5, blocks are uncompressed — build the path shape now, the\ndecompress stage slots in.\n\n# Approach\nPort perf_replay's opt.rs structure: rayon for block-parallel work, mmap for sealed\nartifacts, reusable decode buffers. Planner rule (doc 04): tiny tail -> pointer reads;\nclustered -> coalesce by segment; the filters (previous task) prune candidate segments.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Sealed stream replay >=2.5M ev/s on the reference workload (envelope floor)\n- [ ] Byte-identical results vs naive single-threaded path (checksum gate, always on)\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","perf"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"m","title":"Sealed read paths: coalesced stream replay, parallel global scan, block cache"}	blake3:1l0jgZmkeBTeZJzOJ9NPSoYHpSiofBjrbidrHylkxZc
1783539529903287	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExMA		item.create	bn-2en	{"description":"# Context\nrecovery_scale: full scan is CPU-bound at ~1.3 GiB/s/core -> parallel per-segment scan\nreaches the device ceiling (0.32-0.38 s/GiB); the fast path is O(#sealed) footer preads\n-> the advisory manifest caches footers. Sealed footers provide the A1 contiguity seeds\nthat make per-segment parallelism sound.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Parallel recovery == single-threaded recovery result on the full harness matrix\n- [ ] 4 GiB log: cold full recovery <=0.5 s/GiB; fast path independent of log size\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","perf"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"s","title":"Parallel recovery scan (R1) + manifest footer cache (R2)"}	blake3:wVnA8AgBN4RNUZBMfq96ooL3A0daU9khY0-OOfNknys
1783539530006899	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExMQ		item.create	bn-20b	{"description":"# Context\nThe moment of truth: EventStore switches its default backend from the interim RocksDB\nactor to mess-log + mess-index. Everything from Phases 1-2 must pass unchanged — that's\nthe payoff of API-first.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All Phase 1/2 tests + examples green on the new engine, zero API changes\n- [ ] Bench vs envelope: buffered >=1M ev/s, sealed stream replay >=2.5M ev/s,\n      recovery fast path <=0.5s — recorded in the ledger\n- [ ] Interim backend demoted to a feature flag (kept for differential testing)\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","gate"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"s","title":"Phase 4 exit gate: engine swap + replay/append bench vs envelope"}	blake3:Ha1t_lwHrNeMifiEI-PHeUrdjl-xY54BMoy5Jts8JyE
1783539530111000	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExMg		item.create	bn-1d0	{"description":"# Context\nD4 as resolved by the Phase 0 spec task (G6 head anchor, G10 chain layout). Reference:\nspikes/fold_cert — both verification paths, 13 attack scenarios, measured ~165ns/event\nappend overhead and 0.5us/tail-event verify cost. BLOCKED BY the Phase 0 fold-cert spec\nbone (explicit dep, not just phase ordering).\n\n# Approach\nBLAKE3 chain per the spec's byte-exact encoding (stream_id in genesis — mandatory);\nopt-in per stream/category (D2); chain values materialized per the G10 decision;\nhead anchor per the G6 decision. load_verified() with honest docs (proves prefix\nidentity, not fold-code correctness).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All 13 fold_cert negative tests ported and detected, incl. cross-stream attack\n- [ ] Append overhead with chain on <=5% of composed budget (measured)\n- [ ] Truncation detected even after full index rebuild (the G6 acceptance test)\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","verification"],"parent":"bn-2fc","size":"m","title":"Fold chain + certificates per resolved spec"}	blake3:wsULLlyRLPN6PRLzw0kG3BKkxqIZ3TUV8WoXhCbTvoc
1783539530214302	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExMw		item.create	bn-vy3	{"description":"# Context\nTies Phase 1's derive-generated fold-drift tests to the engine: SnapshotRef.fold_version\nmismatch -> invalidate + rebuild by replay (D4). The derive half exists; this is the\nstore-side enforcement + the invalidation-on-deploy story.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Bumping fold_version invalidates existing snapshots on next load (rebuild, not error)\n- [ ] Metrics count invalidated snapshots (deploy observability)\n","kind":"task","labels":["verification","dx"],"parent":"bn-2fc","size":"s","title":"fold_version enforcement wiring"}	blake3:oC9A4zi8yQbXSH8T4ZfRqp1LHpGAzS6bYuT1jS4W4NM
1783539530328597	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExNA		item.create	bn-1bn	{"description":"# Context\nRound-4 default sealed tier: columnar/128-event blocks/zstd-9/no dicts = 30.8 B/event at\n2.89M ev/s replay, point reads FASTER than row (only needed columns decompress).\nReference: spikes/perf_compress/src/columnar.rs — msgpack tokenizer, per-path columns,\ndelta/varint int columns, interned skeletons, proven on 1M events with 0 mismatches.\n\n# Approach\nPort the shredder/reassembler into mess-index's seal stage as a codec module. The\nreassembly proof is a PERMANENT test mode, not a one-time check: every sealed segment\nverifies a sample (or all, in CI) of events byte-exact before the seal commits.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Byte-exact reassembly on the full reference corpus (1M events, 0 mismatches)\n- [ ] Ratio within 10% of the spike's 30.8 B/event on the same corpus\n- [ ] Unshreddable payloads (non-msgpack, exotic structures) route to row fallback cleanly\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","perf"],"parent":"bn-2fc","size":"m","title":"Columnar shredder + byte-exact reassembly"}	blake3:944JyTs_8tg6nQuXZLrsnU0iS8jM7s5_tG6sBTLakhI
1783539530432727	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExNQ		item.create	bn-zge	{"description":"# Context\nWires the shredder into the staged sealer (Phase 4) per the final D6 format: 1-bit\ncolumnar/row flag in the block index, skeleton tables (~2 KiB/segment) in sealed\nmetadata, 16 KiB dicts ONLY for the row fallback tier (registered via $registry\nDictRegistered), zstd magicless framing.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Mixed segments (columnar + row-fallback blocks) replay correctly (spike proved\n      the format; this proves the integration)\n- [ ] Sealed read paths (Phase 4) hit envelope floors with compression on\n- [ ] dict_id in frames always resolvable via registry (referenced-implies-registered test)\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2fc","size":"m","title":"Sealer integration: columnar default, row fallback, dict registry"}	blake3:rUqn91klnzCgbwsgK6NXzRy7G0HDidZAoWGDa59n3NY
1783539530537136	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExNg		item.create	bn-382	{"description":"# Context\nRound-4 frontier: 26.5 B/event (within 24% of corpus entropy floor) at 0.48M ev/s replay\n— for segments past the replay SLA. An offline re-block of already-sealed segments;\npointer indexes must survive via the logical/physical reference split (doc 02).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Re-blocked segment replays correctly; EventPtrs remain valid (or are rewritten\n      under the same seal-commit discipline)\n- [ ] Opt-in policy config; OFF by default\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage"],"parent":"bn-2fc","size":"s","title":"Archive tier: optional columnar/2048/zstd-19 re-block"}	blake3:2up0vZgaQKAorKw078kzTJAMK6BI6HHX07dAQWXUrjI
1783539530642314	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExNw		item.create	bn-2ug	{"description":"# Context\nfold_cert gap: compaction/retention can delete the frames a verification needs (frame v\nand v+1 around each snapshot). Spec'd in Phase 0; enforced here. v1 retention is\ndelete-whole-segments-only, so the rule is: a segment is retention-blocked while any\nlive SnapshotRef's certification frames live in it (mirrors doc 05's blocker list).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Retention refuses to delete a certification-bearing segment; `retention explain`\n      names the blocking snapshot\n- [ ] Deleting the snapshot unblocks the segment (test)\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","verification"],"parent":"bn-2fc","size":"s","title":"Retention rules: certification-frame preservation"}	blake3:4WlyTm2xJKqlK49hokN6H-w9BAujSAF8tNDn_QjBf1E
1783539530745595	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExOA		item.create	bn-1l6	{"description":"# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Sealed tier <=35 B/event on reference corpus; replay >=2.5M ev/s with columnar on\n- [ ] load_verified round-trips under the crash harness (verify-after-recovery cases)\n- [ ] Ledger updated with compression + verify numbers\n","kind":"task","labels":["gate"],"parent":"bn-2fc","size":"s","title":"Phase 5 exit gate: verification + compression envelope"}	blake3:N58bLz3_cQYHHWhe6KWamc91fiG-pMdvtUwEQoWJ26o
1783539530850100	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExOQ		item.create	bn-4pk	{"description":"# Context\nDoc 12's measured envelope is v1's acceptance target set. This suite turns the per-phase\ngate ledgers into one permanent benchmark run with recorded tolerances (perf work\nwithout a regression ratchet evaporates).\n\n# Approach\ncriterion or custom harness (spikes used custom — fine) over the reference workload;\nJSON ledger committed per run; CI compares against floors with per-metric tolerance\n(e.g. -10%). Interleave + settle-pace per the perf_group_commit finding (sequential\nbenchmarking is invalid on this SSD); document machine-profile assumptions.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] One command runs the suite and produces a pass/fail vs floors\n- [ ] Floors: durable >=100k, buffered >=1M, sealed stream replay >=2.5M, global >=25M,\n      recovery <=0.5 s/GiB, sealed <=35 B/event (tolerances recorded inline)\n","kind":"task","labels":["perf","harness"],"parent":"bn-2sk","size":"m","title":"Envelope regression suite as CI fixture"}	blake3:JnwqVmTgwaQAiup_zibPdmcsT2nC5OP1lrN3E3g7D0Q
1783539530955172	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExOg		item.create	bn-eyy	{"description":"# Context\nDoc 09 Phase 10's operational surface. verify --full is the user-facing face of the\nrecovery scanner; rebuild-index is I5 made executable; retention explain names blockers\n(doc 05); doctor checks lock state, epoch sanity, footer presence, fsync health.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] verify --full detects every corruption class the harnesses inject (reuse harness\n      corruptors as the test)\n- [ ] rebuild-index from bare segments reproduces byte-equal metadata (I5 demo)\n- [ ] Each command has --format json for tooling\n","kind":"task","labels":["ops","dx"],"parent":"bn-2sk","size":"m","title":"mess CLI: doctor, inspect, verify, rebuild-index, retention explain"}	blake3:tiJSB8KU8eix-pYZQ0DjNIxyQoYkumVRt5o8jXOii6Q
1783539531064782	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExOw		item.create	bn-e2y	{"description":"# Context\nRound-4 machine finding: near-full consumer SSDs degrade fdatasync ~50x under sustained\nload (3.3ms -> 150+ms). The store must surface this loudly — it is not optional\n(doc 12 D7 ops note). Plus: subscription lag (D11), block-cache hit rate, seal durations,\nactive-segment age (doc 03 list).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] fsync p50/p95/p99 exposed; degradation past a threshold logs loudly and flags\n      store status\n- [ ] Metrics available programmatically and via `mess inspect`\n","kind":"task","labels":["ops"],"parent":"bn-2sk","size":"s","title":"Runtime metrics: fsync latency (mandatory), lag, cache, seal timings"}	blake3:rd9s8q514O_7zh4IBa-sZoM-3Y7Pri0ZZBjYW0NU48g
1783539531169114	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExPA		item.create	bn-1hl	{"description":"# Context\nThe deliberately-deferred accelerators, admitted only by evidence (doc 12 D10): perfect-\nhash/string-directory experiments for sealed stream lookup (RadixStringSpline vs MPH —\nround-3 said benchmark), and O_DSYNC as an auto-verified device capability (round 4:\n2.3-4.4x on this SSD, but FUA semantics are device-specific — needs a startup probe or\nexplicit opt-in with verification).\n\n# Approach\nEach experiment = a branch + a bench comparison against the envelope suite; merges only\non measured wins (the D10 rule). O_DSYNC probe: write+verify a device-capability check\nat store init before enabling the fast path.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each experiment has a ledger entry with verdict (adopted/rejected + numbers)\n- [ ] O_DSYNC never enabled without the capability probe passing\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","perf"],"parent":"bn-2sk","size":"s","title":"Benchmark-gated experiments: perfect-hash directories, O_DSYNC autodetect"}	blake3:7AMcT25WPdtrZmdIhZrcNlll87RJAv5fJ68y7IS0U2E
