1783542934491443	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExdw		item.create	bn-z98	{"description":"# Context\nDeterministic simulation testing (DST) only works if time, filesystem I/O, and task\nspawning sit behind swappable traits from the FIRST Phase 3 commit — retrofitting after\nthe committer/sealer exist is miserable. This bone is a design constraint disguised as a\ntask: it blocks the segment writer on purpose.\n\n# Approach\nMinimal traits (Clock: now/sleep-until; Fs: open/pwrite/pread/fdatasync/rename; Spawn)\nwith two impls: real (std/tokio) and sim (virtual time, in-memory fs reusing the spike\nfault layers' shape — SectorDisk and FaultWriter both slot behind the Fs trait naturally).\nZero-cost in release: generics or enum dispatch, measured against the perf_append hot\npath to confirm no regression. Do NOT over-abstract — only what mess-log/mess-index\nactually call.\n\n# Reasoning\nmadsim/turmoil were considered; turmoil is network-focused, madsim simulates tokio\nwholesale. A single-node store needs only sim-time + sim-fs + deterministic scheduling of\n~3 actors, so a thin in-house layer that the existing spike fault models plug into beats\nadopting a framework. Revisit if the trait surface grows.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] mess-log compiles and passes its suite against BOTH impls\n- [ ] Hot-path bench shows no measurable regression vs direct calls\n- [ ] SectorDisk (torn_write) and FaultWriter (crash_log) ported behind the Fs trait\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","harness","dst"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"m","title":"Sim-capable runtime abstraction: clock, fs, and spawn traits"}	blake3:_Fm-V_LIv4hOpz3MzBOC6-4e8F1sVBLU-hY5QYwL3Mo
1783542934608734	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExeA		item.create	bn-gux	{"description":"# Context\nThe harnesses inject STRUCTURED faults; coverage-guided fuzzing explores unstructured\nadversarial input. The parser-of-untrusted-bytes surfaces: recovery scanner (arbitrary\ndisk bytes — must never panic, never over-allocate per A2, never accept invalid), opaque\ncursor decoder (CLIENT-supplied input), BatchHeader/subframe decoders, pointer-block +\nskip-table decoders.\n\n# Approach\ncargo-fuzz (libFuzzer) targets per surface; seed corpora from real segments produced by\nthe test suite + the harnesses' interesting cases. CI runs each target briefly per PR\n(regression corpus replay); nightly runs long. Any find becomes a committed regression\ntest in the main suite, not just a corpus entry.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Targets: scan_segment, decode_batch, decode_cursor, decode_ptr_block\n- [ ] Invariants asserted in-target: no panic, allocation bounded by A2 cap, accepted\n      batches re-verify\n- [ ] CI wiring: corpus replay per PR, nightly fuzz job documented\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","harness","fuzzing"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"m","title":"Fuzz targets: recovery scanner, batch/pointer decoders, cursor decode"}	blake3:Lxi3vuex9tMVNzBv2HMxzRNE_3Oh5CojdVxy_QF8aDM
1783542934730410	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExeQ		item.create	bn-y0b	{"description":"# Context\nKani (model checking) proves properties for ALL inputs up to bounds — the right tool for\nsmall pure kernels where sampling gives false comfort: varint/delta pointer encode/decode\nround-trips, skip-table seek bounds, position/watermark arithmetic (overflow), the batch\nacceptance predicate (A1/A5 checks) as a pure function.\n\n# Approach\n#[cfg(kani)] harnesses beside the kernels; keep functions Kani-friendly (no I/O, bounded\nloops — factor them that way in the first place, which is good design pressure). Do NOT\nattempt the committer, async paths, or anything with the fs — wrong tool. Run in CI\nweekly or on-touch (proofs are slow).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Round-trip proofs: ptr delta encode/decode, cursor encode/decode\n- [ ] No-overflow proofs: position arithmetic, watermark advance\n- [ ] Acceptance predicate proof: no input both passes validation and violates the\n      contiguity/empty-batch rules\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","verification"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"s","title":"Kani proof harnesses for pure kernels"}	blake3:6vJwj_3Zc3RDyXCKtMimqN-y4205oQrLYRqv5WDeEcE
1783542934850151	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExeg		item.create	bn-3kn	{"description":"# Context\nThe biggest gap the component harnesses leave: concurrency interleavings (writer racing\nsealer racing subscriber racing crash) currently run on real tokio with real time —\nnon-reproducible. On the sim runtime (Phase 3 abstraction), the store's ~3 actors +\nvirtual time + sim fs give seed-reproducible whole-store scenarios composing fault types\nthe component harnesses can only test separately.\n\n# Approach\nScenario driver: seeded RNG chooses interleavings of {appends, subscriptions at random\ncursors, seal triggers, crashes mid-anything, fsync-EIO injection, slow-consumer stalls};\nvirtual clock eliminates sleeps. Every failure prints its seed; every seed replays\nidentically. Assertions: the D11 gapless invariant, acked-implies-recovered, index==log\nafter every recovery, snapshot law. Start with the sub_handoff + crash_log scenario\ngenerators as templates — they were already seeded and deterministic in spirit.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] 10k+ composed scenarios green in CI (fast profile per PR, full nightly)\n- [ ] A deliberately-injected ordering bug (publish-before-watermark) is caught and its\n      seed replays deterministically (test of the harness)\n- [ ] Crash + subscriber-lag + seal-race composed in a single scenario class\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","harness","dst"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"m","title":"DST harness: seed-reproducible whole-store simulation scenarios"}	blake3:q69keWaTZfgBQJvOGolJ41WAx5BtVX2B5QnybDWC7HY
1783542934976185	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExew		item.create	bn-3az	{"description":"# Context\nThe crash harness's reference model is scoped to the log layer. This is the same idea one\nlevel up: random sequences of PUBLIC API operations (command/append/load/subscribe/\nsave_snapshot, plus crash-recover-reopen) executed against both the real store and a\ntrivially-correct in-memory model, results compared exactly. This is the e2e correctness\nnet for everything the unit tests forgot.\n\n# Approach\nproptest/seeded op-sequence generator; model = Vec-based streams + naive fold; compare\npositions, loaded states, subscription deliveries, error variants (WrongStreamPosition\netc. must match too — error behavior is API). Runs on the sim runtime so crash points are\ndeterministic. Feeds the Phase 4 exit gate (wired as a blocker of bn-20b).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] 5k+ op sequences green incl. crash-reopen mid-sequence\n- [ ] Error-variant equivalence asserted, not just success paths\n- [ ] Shrinking produces a minimal failing op sequence on injected bugs\n","kind":"task","labels":["harness","e2e"],"parent":"bn-2jv","size":"m","title":"Store-level differential test: black-box EventStore vs in-memory model"}	blake3:6_2oVqtF4rhKAZDBHb8WVNbCZmV7TKBCQTgQvkMcvDk
1783542935097091	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExfA		item.create	bn-meo	{"description":"# Context\nPhase 5 adds two new parser surfaces: the columnar reassembler (corrupted column blocks /\nskeleton tables must fail loudly, never reassemble wrong bytes silently — byte-exactness\nis the tier's core promise) and the upcaster/msgpack decode path (old-version payloads,\nhostile inputs).\n\n# Approach\nExtend the Phase 3 cargo-fuzz setup: reassemble_block target (differential — if it\nsucceeds, output must re-shred to the same columns), upcast_stored_event target.\nCorpus seeded from perf_compress's proven corpus.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] reassemble_block: no panic, no silent wrong bytes (differential check in-target)\n- [ ] upcast path: unknown versions/codecs and truncated payloads fail with typed errors\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","fuzzing"],"parent":"bn-2fc","size":"s","title":"Fuzz columnar reassembly + upcaster decode path"}	blake3:heSXJJNXUlXA3KTDlIaQC16T9kZtetNceCFlVYIeGzc
1783542935213998	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExfQ		item.create	bn-yvi	{"description":"# Context\nFor a storage product, on-disk compatibility IS the contract, and nothing currently\nenforces it. Golden store dirs — small, complete stores (segments + sealed artifacts +\nregistry + snapshots) written by each released format version — are committed to the\nrepo; every future version must open, verify, and replay them byte-correctly forever.\n\n# Approach\nA generator writes a canonical golden store (deterministic content — the sim runtime's\nvirtual time helps here); goldens are committed compressed under tests/golden/vN/.\nCI opens every vN with the current code: full recovery scan, registry hydration,\nstream replays vs recorded checksums, load_verified on recorded snapshots. Adding a\nformat change without a new golden (or a migration) fails CI by construction.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] v1 golden committed with recorded checksums; CI job opens + verifies all goldens\n- [ ] A deliberate format tweak without a new golden fails the job (test of the test)\n- [ ] Golden covers: batches, sealed columnar + row-fallback blocks, registry incl.\n      aliases + dicts, snapshots with certificates\n","kind":"task","labels":["e2e","compat"],"parent":"bn-2sk","size":"m","title":"Format-stability e2e: golden store directories"}	blake3:va9aiayMtgkoBopdRwF0kyU7yOkC0805A-6vcjDfD08
1783542935335761	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExfg		item.create	bn-1fo	{"description":"# Context\nSome bugs only exist at hour three: slow leaks, seal/retention churn interactions,\nwatermark drift, fsync degradation on aging devices (the round-4 50x finding), dedupe\nwindow turnover. No current bone runs long.\n\n# Approach\nA soak binary: mixed workload (Zipf writers, subscribers joining/leaving, snapshot\npolicy on, seal + retention churn, periodic SIGKILL-and-recover) with continuous\ninvariant probes (index==log sampling, subscription gap detection, RSS/fd ceilings,\nfsync p99 tracking). Runs nightly for a bounded window (e.g. 2h) and pre-release for\nlonger. Exit criteria are invariants + resource ceilings, not throughput.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] 2h nightly soak green: zero invariant violations, RSS and fd count plateau\n- [ ] Kill-and-recover cycles included; recovery time tracked across the run\n- [ ] Any violation dumps a reproducible state bundle (seed, config, store dir)\n","kind":"task","labels":["e2e","harness"],"parent":"bn-2sk","size":"s","title":"Soak test: multi-hour mixed workload with invariant checking"}	blake3:xUO-9pbrqL_qBI8YFnqr7fOUrmMD46Q9Qtx1D9GWSJg
1783542935457344	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExfw		item.create	bn-13s	{"description":"# Context\nThe torn-write harness empirically checks the commit protocol against ONE fault model\n(independent sector persistence). A formal model checks the protocol's LOGIC against all\nbehaviors a reordering-disk abstraction permits — protocol-level races the harness's\nspecific model can't represent. This is the sweet spot between \"fold associativity is\ntrivially true\" and \"Perennial-scale proof effort\" — model checking, not full proofs.\n\n# Approach\nTLA+ spec of: writer/committer steps, the reordering disk (writes persist as arbitrary\nsubsets between barriers), crash, and the recovery acceptance rules (A1 contiguity, A9\nepoch, A10 no-resync). Check: acked-implies-recovered, no-partial-visible, recovery\nidempotence — with Apalache (or TLC) within small bounds. Lean 4 is an OPTIONAL\ndeepening if the model proves fun: mechanize the same invariants — value is insight,\nnot assurance beyond the model checker. Timeboxed; most valuable run ALONGSIDE Phase 3,\nwhich is why it has no blockers.\n\n# Reasoning\nNot a gate: the 24k-case empirical harness is the conformance bar; this hunts what that\nbar structurally can't see. If the model finds a real protocol issue, it graduates into\nspec changes + harness cases.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Spec covers the D2 protocol incl. A1/A9/A10; invariants checked within stated bounds\n- [ ] Any counterexample becomes a spec fix + a concrete harness case\n- [ ] Findings (or clean bill within bounds) recorded in docs/spec/ as a note\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","verification"],"parent":"bn-3hk","size":"m","title":"Formal model of commit/recovery protocol (TLA+/Apalache; Lean 4 optional)"}	blake3:8qz4cLFziSPKqW2yWEkHjB3bNyG6Y05SOa4dvqjkHAQ
1783542943258809	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExgAE		item.link	bn-ise	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-z98"}	blake3:ID6IENev8EiGkfwKpbzHgppU1I-H4jLt9qrHOQADbPY
