1783544274343994	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExmQE		item.link	bn-2ln	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-eyy"}	blake3:b0uENINdN3U83wE4GRcy-H6LdOgNHuy_ARi8vWiyXnQ
1783544289177535	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExmgE		item.update	bn-1hx	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe measured read designs from spikes/perf_replay: parallel block decompress (95M ev/s\nglobal), segment-order coalesced preads + parallel decompress for streams (24.6M ev/s),\ndecompressed-block LRU (48% hit on replay; useless for random point reads — rely on skip\ntables there). Until Phase 5, blocks are uncompressed — build the path shape now, the\ndecompress stage slots in.\n\n# Approach\nPort perf_replay's opt.rs structure: rayon for block-parallel work, mmap for sealed\nartifacts, reusable decode buffers. Planner rule (doc 04): tiny tail -> pointer reads;\nclustered -> coalesce by segment; the filters (previous task) prune candidate segments.\n\n# mmap SIGBUS stance (required)\nmmap'd sealed files die by SIGBUS — a process crash, not an Err — if the file is truncated\nor the device errors under the mapping. Sealed-segment immutability mostly protects us;\n\"mostly\" is not a policy. Define and test: length-validate against the footer BEFORE\nmapping; map only sealed (never active) files; SIGBUS-class corruption must surface as a\ntyped verify-and-fallback path (or a documented read()-based degraded mode), never a crash.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Sealed stream replay >=2.5M ev/s on the reference workload (envelope floor)\n- [ ] Byte-identical results vs naive single-threaded path (checksum gate, always on)\n- [ ] SIGBUS stance implemented + tested: truncated sealed file yields a typed error and\n      exact-fallback (harness corruptor case), not a process crash"}	blake3:HrZFB6rB6WmxV92qVLJpMh7wOBzeaTmOzYatSmLLGRQ
1783544781839274	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExmwE		item.create	bn-tag	{"description":"# Context & Background\nReads of hot aggregates and the command() retry path both pay a full reload (snapshot + tail\n+ fold) today. Measured: the dx_api spike's 8-writer test did ~1,000 loads for 200 commands —\nevery retry a full reload. This mechanism was designed in the research (doc 02 \"mutable hot\ncache, immutable cold truth\" / doc 05 hot-aggregate caching) and prototyped in spirit by the\nold mess_ecs ComponentStore (quick_cache) — this bone is that idea's proper home; bn-3p5\nretires the ECS vocabulary, this preserves its one good mechanism.\n\n# Goals\ncommand()/load() on a warm aggregate: version-check -> decide -> append (no replay, no fold).\nOn WrongStreamPosition: fetch only the DELTA events since the cached version and fold them in\n— retries become O(events-you-lost-the-race-to) instead of O(full history).\n\n# Reasoning / why this is safe in THIS design\nCache entry = (stream, version, state). Coherence is nearly free: on successful append the\ncaller already holds the events it wrote -> WRITE-THROUGH FOLD into the cached state (no\ninvalidation protocol, no re-read); staleness is detectable by construction via the version\ncheck the append does anyway. In-memory ONLY — never persisted (persisted folded state is\nwhat snapshots are, with certificates). Crash resets it. Durability caveat: under Buffered,\ncached state can reflect acked-but-not-durable events — same documented caveat as D7 cursors.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nLives in mess-store above the Backend trait (works identically on interim RocksDB and the\nreal engine — build once). LRU keyed by stream (quick_cache or moka; the old choice was\nfine), configurable capacity, off-switch. Cache miss falls through to snapshot + tail\n(bn-1pc path). Degraded-store state (D8) taints cache reads the same way it taints direct\nreads. DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED (no measured need): warming, adaptive sizing, persistence,\ncross-process sharing.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Warm command() does zero event reads (instrumented test); conflict retry fetches only\n      the delta (instrumented: events_read == events_lost_race_to)\n- [ ] dx_api-style 8-writer contention bench: total loads collapse vs uncached (record ratio)\n- [ ] Differential oracle: the store-level model test (bn-3az) suite runs cache-on and\n      cache-off with identical results — wired as a standing test mode, not a one-off\n- [ ] Off-switch works; cache-off path is the same code as cache-miss path (no fork)\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Stellar DX\" includes hot-aggregate latency; this is the last piece of the doc-02 FASTER\nadaptation (hot mutable cache over immutable cold truth) and it makes the retry loop cheap\nunder contention — which the north-star command() API silently depends on.","kind":"task","labels":["dx","perf"],"parent":"bn-s2a","size":"m","title":"Aggregate state cache: write-through fold + delta catch-up on conflict"}	blake3:NkWZI2a2n06e2luXaqxwWbnlU0rCDPIQwsw09O-sHys
