1783715996941429	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExigU		item.create	bn-m3z	{"description":"Gap found while answering 'does the social demo use snapshots?': no example exercises the snapshot path at all. social-web opens LogEngine::open(dir) with defaults, every command re-folds from raw events, Projections::new replays the global log from 0, and the README ops-tour shows doctor reporting '[OK] no-snapshots' with a note that the demo never opts in. Deliver: (1) a --snapshots opt-in on social-web (and/or seed) that enables aggregate/fold snapshots on the store; (2) a larger seed tier (e.g. --scale flag, ~50k events) where snapshot-assisted startup measurably beats full replay — record the numbers in the README ops tour; (3) doctor run against the snapshotted store so the fold-version drift check exercises a store that actually HAS snapshots (today that check is vacuously green); (4) keep the rebuild party trick intact and documented — rebuild-from-log must still byte-match the snapshot-assisted state, which is itself the best snapshot-correctness demo. Evidence: examples/social/README.md lines ~116/218-227, src/bin/social-web.rs LogEngine::open defaults.","kind":"task","labels":["dogfood","social"],"title":"social: dogfood the snapshot subsystem (opt-in snapshots + doctor fold-drift on a real store)"}	blake3:xGx_pmZHsI85tJnfrQqW9-LTp9dVVBCfbfwVsm5HAP8
1783717204421195	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExiwU		item.create	bn-gpq	{"description":"Decision (Bob, 2026-07-10): do NOT split social into demo + massive-scale twins — keep ONE example whose model scales, with scale as a seed parameter. Rationale: examples are documentation; a demo-tier crate keeping the likers-set-in-aggregate model would document an anti-pattern, and twin crates sharing 80% code drift under maintenance. A scalable model at N=50 is still small and readable. Shape (4 child bones): (1) remodel likes+follows as per-relationship streams so all aggregate state is bounded; (2) counts become counter projections, and projections get checkpointing so startup resumes from subscribe(from=checkpoint) instead of replaying from 0 — the flagship snapshot story at scale (depends on bn-3mu); (3) writes switch to command_cached, Post/User become Snapshottable, and the retired mega-set Post model survives as a measured hot-post benchmark (command vs command_cached vs snapshot-assisted cold load at ~100k like/unlike events on one post); (4) seed --scale tiers (demo/large ~50k+ events), README ops-tour numbers, and a --rebuild mode that replays from 0 and byte-compares against checkpoint-resumed state (the party trick, upgraded to a snapshot-correctness proof). Side pressure: the large tier explodes stream count (millions of relationship streams), stress-testing the stream directory and mess inspect at app scale (feeds bn-1yz). Supersedes bn-m3z. Context: mega-like-scaling discussion — count accuracy is structural (set-based fold + optimistic concurrency); speed at scale = command_cached warm path (zero reads) + snapshots for cold load; past that, relationship streams are the correct model.","kind":"goal","labels":["social","dogfood"],"title":"social at scale: one example, remodeled so every documented pattern scales"}	blake3:HDbTHMZFTMqbdXMkk7-sQAcOcGnGcMxhnRgjCvOl3fw
1783717215451886	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjAU		item.create	bn-jes	{"description":"Child 1 of bn-gpq. Replace the unbounded HashSet<Id> crowds inside Post.likes and User.follows with tiny per-relationship aggregates: like/<post>/<user> and follow/<follower>/<followee>, each an alternating 0-2-event state machine (Liked/Unliked, Followed/Unfollowed). Rejection pedagogy survives: AlreadyLiked/NotLiked/AlreadyFollowing become the relationship aggregate refusing invalid transitions — keep the doc-comment quality of domain/user.rs (cross-aggregate posture note stays, bn-2v0 references it). Post keeps content + Deleted{by}; User keeps Registered/DisplayNameChanged. Every aggregate now has bounded state — the scaling invariant this goal exists to document. Update domain GWT tests; contracts WriteOps signatures stay but route to the new streams. Note: projections.rs routing must handle the new stream categories (coordinate with bn-19b typed routing if landed). This bone blocks the other three children.","kind":"task","labels":["social"],"parent":"bn-gpq","size":"m","title":"social: remodel likes + follows as per-relationship streams (bounded aggregate state)"}	blake3:_hRcwPilju88A5thFHq4R7kLsDwfByVdp8ftlp5rCeY
1783717227548387	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjQU		item.create	bn-3vm	{"description":"Child 2 of bn-gpq. (a) Like/follower counts become counter projections over the relationship streams (reads are already projection-backed; this relocates count logic out of aggregate state). (b) Projections gain a persisted checkpoint (position + projection state, versioned like fold_version so a projection-logic change invalidates the checkpoint and forces clean rebuild) so startup resumes from EventStore::subscribe(from=checkpoint) instead of replaying the global log from 0 — at a 50k+ event tier replay-from-0 per boot is the thing that breaks, and this is the flagship snapshot story at scale. Uses bn-3mu's subscription API (blocker): live pump switches from the 1ms polling adapter to the real catch-up->live subscription with await_past replacing the poll-bounded wait_for. Keep the pull-style query-time-filtering design where it stands (rebuild==live by construction); checkpoint the indexes it maintains. Tests: kill+restart resumes from checkpoint and serves identical pages; stale-version checkpoint triggers full rebuild; anomaly-counter behavior (bn-3uu) preserved.","kind":"task","labels":["social"],"parent":"bn-gpq","size":"m","title":"social: counter projections + checkpointed projections (resume via subscribe(from), not replay-from-0)"}	blake3:CNqDlUKWKQ5RR6yd0BX6vMS-yA_xaMcOzAKa0CN6k3o
1783717241560241	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjgU		item.create	bn-22v	{"description":"Child 3 of bn-gpq. (a) Switch all WriteOps paths from EventStore::command to command_cached (warm path: version-check -> decide -> append, zero reads) and implement Snapshottable (+Clone) for the post-remodel aggregates so the miss path is the snapshot+tail load_cached. This is the first real dogfooding of both mechanisms. (b) Preserve the retired unbounded-likers-set Post model as a benchmark-only module (not the shipped design): seed ONE post stream with ~100k like/unlike events (~30% unlikes) and print a table comparing per-command latency of (1) plain command full replay, (2) command_cached warm path, (3) snapshot-assisted cold load after cache drop — plus snapshot size vs replayed-event count to show churn compaction (state shrinks with unlikes while history grows). Real fs under the TMPDIR rule, never tmpfs; use the self-sweeping temp helper if bn-cxr has landed. Numbers land in the README ops tour (child 4 consumes them). This answers 'is liking still fast at 1M likes and how do snapshots play in' with measurements instead of prose.","kind":"task","labels":["social","dogfood"],"parent":"bn-gpq","size":"m","title":"social: command_cached + Snapshottable writes, and the mega-set Post kept as a measured hot-post benchmark"}	blake3:T2yx9NIbVvR6LAGZEvFkoX0DAuWNhgr2Ji4uADGrfZg
1783717255516971	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjwU		item.create	bn-o9z	{"description":"Child 4 of bn-gpq. (a) seed gains --scale demo|large: demo stays the current deterministic 1,488-event tier; large is ~50k+ events with realistic skew (a few hot posts with thousands of likes, celebrity users with thousands of followers) — still deterministic (seeded RNG), still one command per action, but use join_all pipelining over distinct relationship streams (safe under the per-stream gate; cite bn-162's guidance if landed). (b) social-web gains --rebuild: replay the global log from 0 into a fresh projection and byte-compare against the checkpoint-resumed state — the party trick upgraded into a snapshot/checkpoint-correctness proof, exercised in CI at demo scale and documented for large. (c) README ops tour re-run at the large tier with real numbers: seed wall-clock, checkpoint-resume vs replay-from-0 startup, hot-post benchmark table from bn-22v, mess inspect against a store with tens of thousands of relationship streams (stress for bn-1yz pagination), doctor against a store that actually has snapshots (fold-drift check no longer vacuously green — the original bn-m3z ask). Depends on bn-3vm + bn-22v.","kind":"task","labels":["social"],"parent":"bn-gpq","size":"s","title":"social: seed --scale tiers, README ops-tour numbers, and --rebuild byte-compare mode"}	blake3:vhBM4rhNoUgZGCpCw6TCePBZnWa-Mgx-rOlleV-e1c4
